      "text": "Given that the fifth term of an arithmetic sequence is \\(11\\) and the common difference between two consecutive terms is \\(1\\), we aim to find the product of the first two terms.\n\n### Step 1: Determine the difference up to the fifth term\n\nIn an arithmetic sequence, each term increases by a constant difference. Here, the common difference \\(d\\) is \\(1\\). Therefore, to find the fifth term, we add the common difference \\(d\\) four times to the first term.\n\nLet \\(a\\) be the first term. The formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is:\n\\[\na_n = a + (n-1)d\n\\]\nFor the fifth term (\\(a_5\\)):\n\\[\na_5 = a + (5-1)d = a + 4d\n\\]\nGiven \\(a_5 = 11\\) and \\(d = 1\\):\n\\[\n11 = a + 4 \\cdot 1 \\implies 11 = a + 4 \\implies a = 11 - 4 \\implies a = 7\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the product of the first two terms\n\nThe first term \\(a\\) is \\(7\\). The second term can be calculated by adding the common difference \\(d\\):\n\\[\nb = a + d = 7 + 1 = 8\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Find the product of the first two terms\n\n\\[\n\\text{Product} = a \\cdot b = 7 \\cdot 8 = 56\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the first two terms is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{56}\n\\]",
