      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the first two terms of the arithmetic sequence and then find their product.\n\n1. **Identify the formula for the \\( n \\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:**\n   The \\( n \\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   a_n = a_1 + (n-1) \\cdot d\n   \\]\n   where \\( a_n \\) is the \\( n \\)-th term, \\( a_1 \\) is the first term, \\( d \\) is the common difference, and \\( n \\) is the term number.\n\n2. **Use the given information:**\n   We are given that the fifth term is 11. Therefore, we can write:\n   \\[\n   a_5 = 11\n   \\]\n   According to the formula, the fifth term is:\n   \\[\n   a_5 = a_1 + (5-1) \\cdot d = a_1 + 4d\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given value:\n   \\[\n   a_1 + 4d = 11\n   \\]\n\n3. **Use the difference between two consecutive terms:**\n   We are also given that the difference between two consecutive terms is 1. Therefore, we can write:\n   \\[\n   a_2 - a_1 = 1\n   \\]\n   According to the formula, this is:\n   \\[\n   (a_1 + d) - a_1 = 1\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   d = 1\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute \\( d \\) back into the equation for the fifth term:**\n   We already have:\n   \\[\n   a_1 + 4d = 11\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( d = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   a_1 + 4 \\cdot 1 = 11\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   a_1 + 4 = 11\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( a_1 \\):\n   \\[\n   a_1 = 11 - 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a_1 = 7\n   \\]\n\n5. **Determine the second term:**\n   Using the common difference \\( d = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   a_2 = a_1 + d = 7 + 1 = 8\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculate the product of the first two terms:**\n   The product of the first two terms is:\n   \\[\n   a_1 \\cdot a_2 = 7 \\cdot 8 = 56\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the first two terms is \\(\\boxed{56}\\).",
